# Explicit concept ledger. Source defects remain flagged, not silently taught.
C('framework',174,'ROS as an open-source robotics framework','ROS supplies tools, libraries, conventions and middleware rather than replacing every host operating-system responsibility.')
C('ecosystem',174,'Ecosystem, licensing and platform claims','The slide advertises community packages, permissive licensing and ROS 2 platform support. Package/user counts are historical source claims, not live measurements.')
C('timeline',175,'Historical release timeline requiring adjudication','The timeline combines a 2017 row with Crystal and first-stable wording and labels Jazzy latest. Exact chronology and current status are not accepted without adjudication.',status='flagged')
C('capabilities',176,'ROS 2 design capabilities','The six cards associate multi-robot operation, embedded deployment, real-time control, reliability, security and vendor-neutral DDS with ROS 2. Capabilities do not establish configured guarantees.')
C('dds',176,'Vendor-neutral communication middleware','The slide associates DDS with vendor-neutral distributed communication, not with a specific motor or visualization device.')
C('layers',177,'Hosted robotics software stack','In the shown stack, hardware supports Ubuntu, ROS middleware supports application software, and Python AI/navigation belongs to the application layer.')
C('ipc',178,'Interprocess communication analogy','Pipes represent communication infrastructure; rooms are programs and water represents exchanged sensor or command data.')
C('modularity',179,'Modular Open Systems Approach','Standardized interfaces allow modules to be developed, tested and reused separately when their interfaces are compatible.')
C('decoupling',180,'Different perception and control rates','Slow perception should not impose its computation period on a separate faster control or safety loop; separation is a design mechanism, not a safety proof.')
C('standard-messages',[180,190],'Interoperable structured messages','AI and drivers exchange explicitly typed structured messages; an interface must agree on representation rather than rely on arbitrary unstructured payloads.')
C('jetson',181,'Jetson as the course example host','The course assigns GPU-assisted vision and high-level middleware to Jetson and simpler motor-control timing to a microcontroller. This is not a universal requirement.')
C('primitives',182,'Distinct ROS communication and organization primitives','Nodes compute, topics stream, services exchange requests/responses, actions handle goal-oriented work, messages specify data, and packages organize files.')
C('node',183,'Single-purpose computational nodes','Separate camera acquisition, object detection and motor-control responsibilities illustrate the node abstraction without requiring every deployment to use exactly one process per node.')
C('isolation',184,'Process isolation and scheduling scope','In the depicted separate-process deployment, one process failing need not directly terminate another. It does not guarantee correct control, safe stopping or automatic optimal CPU/GPU scheduling.')
C('package',185,'Package versus runtime node','Packages organize source, configuration and data; nodes are running computations. Folder organization is not itself execution.')
C('graph-camera',186,'Directed camera-to-detection edge','The explicit graph sends /camera/raw from Camera Node to AI Detection; it does not use the /image_raw name from a different example.','ROS graph and code')
C('graph-objects',186,'Detection-to-navigation edge','AI Detection publishes /detected_objects to Navigation before Navigation supplies /cmd_vel to Drive.','ROS graph and code')
C('graph-control',186,'Navigation-to-drive velocity edge','The graph separates perception output from drive commands and gives each edge its own topic.','ROS graph and code')
C('rclpy-lifecycle',187,'Minimal rclpy lifecycle','The displayed main initializes rclpy, constructs MyNode, spins it and then calls shutdown. Spin services callbacks rather than mechanically rotating the robot.','ROS graph and code')
C('node-name',187,'Class identity versus ROS node name','MyNode is the Python class; my_node is the node name passed to the base-class initializer.','ROS graph and code')
C('pubsub',188,'Asynchronous publish-subscribe','A camera publisher feeds an image topic and subscribers receive callbacks without an application-level request/response for every frame.','Topics and messages')
C('many-to-many',[188,189],'Topic fan-out and multiple participants','Topics support multiple publishers and subscribers. The radio example fans one topic out to three receivers; it does not impose one-to-one pairing.','Topics and messages')
C('rates',[188,191,192],'Publication period and frequency','The camera illustration uses 30 Hz; the code timer uses 0.1 seconds, or 10 Hz. Ideal event counts require stated interval and timing assumptions.','Topics and messages')
C('radio',[189,190],'Topic-name and message-type agreement','The radio analogy highlights agreeing on channel name and payload type. It is not a complete guarantee of network, discovery or QoS compatibility.','Topics and messages')
C('image-twist',190,'Image data versus velocity commands','sensor_msgs/Image carries image data; geometry_msgs/Twist expresses linear and angular velocity components.','Topics and messages')
C('callbacks',[191,192],'Timer callbacks versus subscription callbacks','The publisher timer schedules publish_msg; message arrival schedules the subscription callback. Merely creating a subscription is not a periodic publisher.','ROS graph and code')
C('pubsub-code',192,'PubSubNode code structure','One displayed node creates a String publisher and subscriber on topic, publishes Hello ROS on a 0.1-second timer, and prints received data in callback.','ROS graph and code')
C('service',193,'On-demand request-response services','Services pair a request with a response for discrete work rather than continuously broadcast sensor streams.','Services, actions and parameters')
C('service-caveat',[193,194,199],'Service completion and synchrony caveat','Guaranteed response and universally synchronous wording are too strong; the later example explicitly uses call_async. Network availability is distinct from application completion.','Services, actions and parameters',status='flagged')
C('client-server',[194,195],'Client/server direction','A client initiates a request and the server produces a response; the get_map diagram sends an OccupancyGrid response from Map Node back to Nav Node.','Services, actions and parameters')
C('action',196,'Long-running goal-oriented actions','Actions expose a goal, acceptance/rejection, progress feedback, a terminal result and cancellation interaction, illustrated by navigate_to_pose.','Services, actions and parameters')
C('action-internals',196,'Action transport simplification requiring adjudication','The slide claims three topics internally despite ROS 2 framing. Logical goal/feedback/result fields must not be confused with an exhaustive ROS 2 transport inventory.','Services, actions and parameters',status='flagged')
C('cancel',196,'Cancellation request versus inevitable cancellation','The cancellation capability does not imply every request is accepted or that a completed goal can always be cancelled. The figure boxes are not a mandatory chronological sequence.','Services, actions and parameters')
C('parameters',[197,198],'Node configuration parameters','Parameters hold named configuration values, with declared defaults and later reads. Runtime adjustment affects behavior only when supported and consumed by the node.','Services, actions and parameters')
C('availability',[198,199],'Service availability versus response readiness','wait_for_service checks availability; call_async initiates a request and returns a future. A visible service is not proof that a result has already arrived.','ROS graph and code')
C('add-service',199,'AddTwoInts handler and request values','The handler assigns request.a + request.b to response.sum. The shown request uses 2 and 3, so a completed handler produces 5.','ROS graph and code')
C('speed-param',199,'Independent speed parameter read','The speed default is 1.0 and the snippet prints that parameter; it does not print the service sum or read future.result().','ROS graph and code')
C('sensing',200,'Sensor-to-perception dataflow','Drivers publish raw sensor data; perception subscribes and may use the GPU; decision logic turns perceived state into movement choices.','Robot dataflow')
C('actuation',201,'High-level commands versus low-level motor timing','Jetson performs high-level computation and sends commands; the microcontroller handles precise PWM timing and a driver stage powers motors.','Robot dataflow')
C('hardware-chain',[200,201],'End-to-end sensing-to-motion chain','The shown chain separates a camera, typed ROS image messages, Jetson processing, serial command transfer, microcontroller/motor shield, H-bridge and DC motors.','Robot dataflow')
C('urdf',202,'URDF robot description','URDF is an XML description of robot structure and properties, not an online perception stream.','Robot description and transforms')
C('links',202,'Rigid links and link properties','Links represent rigid parts with visual appearance, collision geometry and inertial properties. Those properties serve different purposes.','Robot description and transforms')
C('joints',202,'Fixed, continuous and revolute joints','The examples pair a fixed camera mount, continuously turning wheel and revolute arm hinge with different joint types.','Robot description and transforms')
C('tf',203,'Relations between coordinate frames','TF represents relations among robot coordinate frames, allowing sensor data to be interpreted relative to other parts or a world frame when valid transforms are available.','Robot description and transforms')
C('tf-tree',203,'Frame tree parent/child structure','In the illustrated tree camera_link is a child of base_link; each non-root frame has one parent but may have several children. The root is the exception.','Robot description and transforms')
C('urdf-tf',[202,203],'Static description versus changing pose','URDF describes structure; live transform computation combines that description with current state. A fixed relative transform can remain unchanged as the robot moves globally.','Robot description and transforms')
C('state-publisher',204,'robot_state_publisher inputs and output','robot_description supplies structure and /joint_states supplies current joint state; forward kinematics produces frame transforms.','Robot description and transforms')
C('tf-not-map',204,'Transform publication is not physics or occupancy mapping','The diagram label live 3D map refers to frame relations, not a newly built occupancy map or a Gazebo physics result. Fixed-transform transport is omitted in the slide.','Robot description and transforms')
C('sim-real',[205,206],'Simulation-to-real interface reuse','A stable interface can allow the same computation to use a simulation bridge or hardware driver; physical behavior still requires validation.','Simulation and visualization')
C('rviz-vs-gazebo',[205,207,208],'Visualization versus physical simulation','RViz visualizes ROS data and models; Gazebo simulates physical interactions and sensors. Neither label makes the tools interchangeable.','Simulation and visualization')
C('adapter',206,'Replace the adapter, preserve the contract','The example swaps Simulation Bridge and Hardware Driver while retaining /cmd_vel, rather than rewriting every navigation operation.','Simulation and visualization')
C('rviz-sensors',207,'RViz sensor displays','PointCloud2, LaserScan and Image display 3D point clouds, 2D scans and camera imagery respectively.','Simulation and visualization')
C('rviz-spatial',207,'RViz model, transform and plan displays','TF Frames, RobotModel and Path display frame relationships, URDF robot geometry and planned trajectories respectively.','Simulation and visualization')
C('rviz-markers',207,'RViz annotations and map display','MarkerArray supports custom annotations; the slide also shows OccupancyGrid as a mapping display.','Simulation and visualization')
C('occupancy-dimension',207,'OccupancyGrid dimensionality requires correction','The card says 2D or 3D maps. Questions do not adopt that as the standard OccupancyGrid interface dimensionality.','Simulation and visualization',status='flagged')
C('gazebo-physics',208,'Gazebo physics and simulated sensors','Rigid-body interactions, friction, collisions and sensor models support simulation; fidelity depends on configured models.','Simulation and visualization')
C('bridge',208,'Bidirectional ros_gz communication bridge','The bridge connects configured Gazebo topics and ROS 2 topics in the required direction; simulation messages are not automatically every ROS interface.','Simulation and visualization')
C('sdf',208,'SDF worlds and models','The Gazebo slide associates SDF with simulated environments and model descriptions rather than ROS parameter retrieval or live TF updates.','Simulation and visualization')
C('hil',208,'Hardware-in-the-loop','HIL combines real hardware with simulated components rather than requiring every component to be simulated.','Simulation and visualization')
C('gazebo-versions',208,'Version-sensitive Gazebo terminology','Physics-engine and release-name examples mix version-specific capabilities and must not serve as a current installation or universal support matrix.','Simulation and visualization',status='flagged')
C('integration',209,'Sense-think-act with modular communication','The summary combines sensing, AI decisions, actuation, communication primitives and body-space representation without proving timing or safety guarantees.','Robot dataflow')
